Fifteenth system tracing expansion (2026-10-13)
==============================================

Scope: PF cookies/table transactions and pfsync; NVMe recovery; ZFS scan and
rebuild; USB exploration and PCI deferred hotplug; sockstat, ipcs, gzip, tar.
39 distinct contracts: 30 kernel SDT and 9 user USDT. Ten profiles bring the
installed profile catalog to 414 entries. Profiles are installed by the
existing bsdinstruments Makefile in /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles.
No software was installed, no modules loaded, and no machine rebooted.

Contract inventory: coverage-fifteenth.json. Its kernel argument_expressions
show one representative call site, not all possible values. The semantics
below cover every site. Repeated call sites are not counted as new contracts.
All 39 are disjoint from the pre-pass whole-tree 3,084-contract inventory at
/usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-census-20261013/working-tree.json. This is source coverage,
not a claim about the number registered on a particular running kernel.

Kernel contracts: sdt:::NAME
---------------------------
Arguments are zero based; pointers identify objects only, not stable IDs.
Do not dereference retained pointers or assume they survive the event.

pf-syncookie-active: active boolean, configured mode. After the always-mode
assignment, adaptive activation, or rotation-triggered deactivation. A mode
assignment may repeat the active value; no key/cookie/address data is exposed.

pf-table-begin / pf-table-rollback: flags, affected inactive-table count,
zero status, at the successful transaction-processing exit. These do not fire
for rejected flags, failed ruleset allocation, or rollback's no-op exit.
pf-table-commit: flags, added+changed table count, status (0 or EBUSY for an
invalid/closed ticket). Dummy transactions retain PFR_FLAG_DUMMY and do not
imply any change was applied. Table names and addresses are omitted.

pfsync-queue-pressure: bucket ID, existing packet bytes, requested extra bytes
before an MTU-triggered sendout, at both queue insertion paths. Not queue count.
pfsync-bulk-start: softc identity, after accepting an outgoing bulk request.
pfsync-bulk-status-send: softc, PFSYNC_BUS_* status before constructing/sending
that status message. This is an attempt, not confirmation of network delivery.
pfsync-bulk-retry / pfsync-bulk-failed: incremented attempt count in the bulk
receive watchdog. Failed is emitted before the existing reset/pretend-OK logic;
it explicitly does not claim successful synchronization.

nvme-watchdog-reset: qpair identity, sampled CSTS, only when reset was accepted.
nvme-request-expired: qpair, local tracker CID, whether an abort was already
sent, after completion polling still leaves an expired command.
nvme-abort-pressure: qpair, reason 1=ACL capacity, 2=abort submission failure.
nvme-abort-sent: qpair, local tracker CID, after successful abort submission.
Local CIDs exclude the hardware cid_base offset. Qpair identity is required
for correlation; neither opcode payloads nor namespace addresses are exposed.
nvme-queued-resubmit: qpair, opcode, retry count before re-enabling queued I/O.
It marks a submission attempt, not completion or a guaranteed hardware retry.
These probes run in watchdog/recovery paths, not completion, panic polling,
raw interrupt, or early startup paths. They are guarded by !cold.

scan-state: scan identity, DSS_SCANNING, txg during scan setup.
scan-done: scan identity, DSS_FINISHED or DSS_CANCELED, scan errors, txg.
scan-paused: scan identity, 1=paused or 0=resumed after changing scrub flags.
scan-checkpoint: scan identity, txg after queue/state checkpoint updates in
sync processing. This does not mean the txg is already durable on disk.
These cover the normal scrub/resilver scan; error-scrub has a separate state
machine and is not represented by these events.
rebuild-state: vdev identity, VDEV_REBUILD_ACTIVE/COMPLETE/CANCELED, txg.
rebuild-wait-start: rebuild identity, kind (1=inflight capacity, 2=final drain),
inflight bytes; rebuild-wait-done: identity, kind. Each cv_wait is paired,
including spurious wakes; repeated waits are not one continuous operation.
All new portable ZFS calls use trace_bsd.h: no argument evaluation in libzpool
or foreign tracing backends, and no undeclared Linux tracepoint additions.

usb-attach-done: child identity, usb_error_t from the three explore/reconfigure
probe-and-attach calls. It does not observe every driver attachment globally.
usb-disconnect: parent identity, port before freeing an existing or failed
child in reattachment. It includes error cleanup, not just physical unplug.
usb-enumerate-start: parent, port; usb-enumerate-done: parent, port, allocation
success boolean after usb_alloc_device. No invented errno for NULL results.
usb-tt-drain-start/done: hub identity around translator-message drain wait.
These run after cold startup and are excluded from USB_GLOBAL_INCLUDE_FILE
builds; TT probes additionally require USB_HAVE_TT_SUPPORT.

pci-hotplug-start/done: bridge softc, child-present boolean around the locked,
deferred hotplug task. Child presence is not attachment success or an errno.
pci-hotplug-watchdog: bridge, kind 1=command completion/2=DLL active, sampled
slot/link status. Both timeout and missed-interrupt recovery branches are
observable from the register bits. No extra hardware reads are introduced.
Probes are !cold guarded and do not run in the raw PCI interrupt handler.

User contracts
--------------
USDT is selected only with MK_DTRACE != no, excluding RESCUE and BOOTSTRAPPING.
Headers guard argument evaluation and preserve errno. Off builds use original
calls directly. Shared ipc.c also compiles in ipcrm with tracing disabled.
Portable bsdtar code needs no generated provider header in a non-USDT build.

socketsnapshot*:::query-start / ipcsnapshot*:::query-start:
  sizing-only boolean, requested bytes (0 for a sizing-only query).
query-done: return value, errno on -1 (otherwise 0), resulting bytes on success
(otherwise 0). Socket probes wrap cap_sysctlbyname, IPC probes sysctlbyname.
No sysctl names, socket addresses, process names, IDs or returned structs are
recorded. IPC's kvm backend and sockstat's numeric-MIB calls are not covered.

gzipstream*:::inflate-start: available input/output bytes before inflate.
inflate-done: zlib result and remaining input/output bytes. Z_BUF_ERROR with
Z_FINISH can be normal; classify by the caller's state, not just negativity.
decode-error: gzip state enum, input bytes read, at stop_and_fail before its
failure result is assigned. Includes framing, CRC, length, truncation and
stream failures reaching that exit, but not every early fatal allocation or
other compression format. State values: 0/1 magic, 2 method, 3 flags, 4 skip,
5..7 extra, 8 original name, 9 comment, 10/11 header CRC, 12 init, 13 inflate,
14 payload CRC, 15 payload length. Neither CRC values nor contents are exposed.

archiveop*:::operation-start: operation 1=next-header, 2=extract-entry;
operation-done: operation, libarchive status. ARCHIVE_EOF is not an error;
negative statuses have libarchive semantics, not errno semantics. These cover
bsdtar's read/extract entry loop, not all archive library API calls or writes.
No paths, archive entry names, ownership or payloads are exposed.

Profiles and use
----------------
pf-table-cookie-events, pfsync-recovery-events, nvme-watchdog-events,
zfs-scan-progress, zfs-rebuild-progress, usb-enumeration-latency,
pci-hotplug-recovery, base-snapshot-latency, base-inflate-results,
base-archive-operations.

Example: bsdinstruments generate -f \
  /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles/base-inflate-results.d --pid PID
Start paired profiles before the workload. Lost events invalidate latency
pairing. Times include scheduling and preemption. Thread-local nesting is
cleared on matched returns. PID filters can omit deferred kernel work.
These profiles never dereference kernel identities or read payloads.

Validation and limitations
--------------------------
Artifacts and exact per-file originals:
  /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-fifteenth-validation
Maintained checks: coverage-fifteenth-{compile,modules,libzpool,base,check,
codegen,guards,zfs-guards,integration,smoke}.py in this directory.
They use the native prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG tree and standard /usr/obj root;
compile flags are obtained from the kernel/module Makefiles, including
per-source ZFS flags. Parallel compilation uses all logical CPUs.
codegen additionally requires the preserved before/ sources in the artifact.

Results:
- Eight kernel sources, tracing enabled/disabled: 16 native Werror compiles.
- PF, pfsync, ZFS, NVMe and USB modules link; prepared kernel links.
- Both changed portable ZFS sources compile in base libzpool.
- Four base tools plus shared-source ipcrm build in both modes: ten builds.
- Exact kernel definitions have executable SDT NOP sites; off objects omit them.
- Four user binaries have correct DOF arities and enabled offsets; off binaries
  omit the new providers. These checks do not establish live registration.
- Twenty profile renders/offline compiles (plain and PID/duration filtered),
  with separate selector/argument checks and install-catalog membership.
- Eight kernel sources have identical normalized no-hooks .text bytes to
  originals. This compares code bytes, not relocation equivalence or runtime.
- Eight real-header guard/errno fixtures, three ZFS backend guard fixtures,
  and 21 source/build-selection/USB alternate-guard integration checks.
- 36 real binary behavior checks: gzip valid/concatenated/empty compression,
  CRC/truncation/header errors; tar list/extract/bad/missing archives;
  IPv4/IPv6/Unix socket snapshots and shared-memory/queue/semaphore snapshots.

Host limitation: sockstat -l (unqualified protocols) fails through Casper with
"Socket is not connected" in both builds and installed /usr/bin/sockstat.
The -46l, -46 and -u snapshot checks pass. No tracing-induced behavior change
was identified; this pre-existing host path is not diagnosed here.

/dev/dtrace is unavailable. No live probe firing, driver fault injection,
pool scrub/rebuild, PF reconfiguration, or hardware hotplug was performed.
No live kernel event ordering or tracing overhead qualification is claimed.

Update 2026-10-15: FreeBSD pf was retired when OpenBSD PF landed.  The PF
contracts above keep their names and arguments and now fire from
contrib/openbsd-pf/sys/net (marked "5BSD: DTrace"); see dtrace_pf(4).
